\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The finite-chain kinetic normalization}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Compare the finite spacetime action with the
[interacting slice transfer](fdip-00GU). Let spatial spacing be
#{a}, time spacing #{b}, and choose site coordinates
#{q_j=\sqrt a\,\phi_j}. The temporal kinetic term is
##{\frac a{2b}\sum_j(\phi_{j,k+1}-\phi_{j,k})^2
            =\frac1{2b}\|q_{k+1}-q_k\|^2.}
At a single time slice the spatial and mass terms become
##{b\left[\frac1{2a^2}\sum_j(q_{j+1}-q_j)^2
                       +\frac{m_0^2}{2}\sum_jq_j^2\right],}
and the Wick quartic becomes
##{b\sum_j\left[\frac{\lambda q_j^4}{24a}
       -\frac{\lambda c q_j^2}{4}+\frac{\lambda a c^2}{8}\right].}
These are direct substitutions in the finite action of
\ref{fdip-00HZ}. Boundary bonds contribute their zero-exterior
terms exactly as there. A Dirichlet time boundary gives endpoint
Gaussian kernels; the [periodic thermal trace](fdip-00HB) has different
time-boundary data.}

\p{The factor #{1/(2b)} agrees with the heat normalization in
\citet{(1.2)}{simon1999feynman} and the transfer in
[the slice construction](fdip-00GU). The isotropic GRS limit takes
#{a=b=\epsilon\to0} at fixed spacetime square. The algebraic
matching above does not establish a time-first limit at fixed
#{a}, a periodic-trace limit, or a simultaneous mesh/volume limit.}
